<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Model and training</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement all RNNs, meaning the gating network and each of the module networks, as simple Elman RNNs, each with a <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional single-layer hidden state. The control RNN in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.3 One-shot task acquisition ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>a,b is a GRU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho et al., 2014</a>)</cite> with <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7*32=224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7*32=224</annotation></semantics></math> hidden units to match the overall number in our model (but with many more weights, as there are no independent modules). The initial hidden state of these networks is a learned parameter vector. At each timestep, a linear projection from the gating hidden state parameterises a categorical probability distribution by assigning log probabilities to each category (Equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). We sample the selected module from these probabilities through the gumbel-softmax reparameterisation trick <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jang et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. That yields a soft (i.e. approximately but not strictly one-hot) activation vector across modules, which allows for gradient flow through the sampling step during training. After training, during inference on test tasks, we replace the activation vector by its hard (i.e. strictly one-hot in the argmax dimension) equivalent. To obtain the timestep’s module hidden state, we sum the hidden state across all modules, weighted by the activation vector. A linear projection from the resulting module hidden state provides the output at the timestep (Equation <a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Training the gating and module parameters simultaneously is prone to local minima and instability. To mitigate this, we found the initialisation of network weights to be important. Small initial weights, determined by weight factor <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{init}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{init}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{init}=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{init}=0.001</annotation></semantics></math> for motor learning), decrease variability across seeds. Specifically, we initialise linear layer weights as Xavier uniform with gain <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{init}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{init}</annotation></semantics></math> and initialise linear layer bias (if present) as <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. For recurrent networks, we initialise input weights as Xavier uniform with gain <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{init}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{init}</annotation></semantics></math> and bias as <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>; recurrent weights as orthogonal, then multiplied by <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{init}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{init}</annotation></semantics></math>; and recurrent bias as <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. We then train our model’s parameters by gradient descent on the negative marginal log likelihood (Equation <a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) using the ADAM optimiser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma &amp; Ba, 2017</a>)</cite> with learning rate <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0003" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0003</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0003</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0001</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0001</annotation></semantics></math> for motor learning) for <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>k iterations. We obtain this loss through particle filtering (Equations <a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>-<a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>; also see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Probabilistic inference ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>) with <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="250" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>250</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">250</annotation></semantics></math> particles. We use a batch size of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math>, gradient clipping of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, without regularisation or learning rate schedulers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Probabilistic inference</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use particle filtering to implement probabilistic inference in our learned generative model. Each particle carries the module hidden state <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{m}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒎</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{m}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the gating hidden state <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{g}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{g}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as the selected module <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textnormal{z}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{z}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Tracking the RNN hidden states matters, because these contain long-range history dependencies; particles with the same <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textnormal{z}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{z}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> may have different <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{m}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒎</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{m}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{g}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{g}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> because their module selection history differs. Additionally, we track each particle’s ancestor. After updating the particle parameters following Equations <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the ancestor indexes which particle the current particle was resampled from in Equation <a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. That allows us to trace back the sequence of module activations with the highest likelihood at the end of the episode <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Douc et al., 2009</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are two technical details that majorly improve the efficiency of the particle resampling procedure described in Equations <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. The first is that we use stratified (also called balanced or systematic) resampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kitagawa, 1996</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doucet &amp; Johansen, 2009</a>)</cite> to implement Equation <a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Equation <a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> defines a multinomial distribution that we repeatedly sample from to update the particle system with respect to the likelihood of the current observations. But rather than sampling from that distribution directly, we sample <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textnormal{u}}\sim\text{Uniform}(0,1/K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>u</mtext><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Uniform</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{u}}\sim\text{Uniform}(0,1/K)</annotation></semantics></math>, and then follow a deterministic procedure for obtaining particles for the next step. We define a set of boundaries <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as the cumulative sum of the particle probabilities in Equation <a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>: <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}=\sum_{i=1}^{k}l_{t}^{(k)}/\sum_{j=1}^{K}l_{t}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}=\sum_{i=1}^{k}l_{t}^{(k)}/\sum_{j=1}^{K}l_{t}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we select resampled particles by taking steps <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\{u+i/K\}_{i=0...(K-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\{u+i/K\}_{i=0...(K-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and returning the particles for which <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k-1}\leq b&lt;b_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k-1}\leq b&lt;b_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. This stratified sampling reduces degeneracy, where many particles carry identical information.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second improvement to particle resampling efficiency only applies when training the model. That is because this method, referred to as guided particle filtering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carpenter et al., 1999</a>)</cite>, relies on observations in the future. During inference, we assume our model doesn’t have access to these future observations, so we stick to Equations <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (known as bootstrap particle filtering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doucet et al., 2001</a>)</cite>). In guided particle filtering, we sample particle module selection <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textnormal{z}}_{t}\sim p({\textnormal{z}}_{t}|{\textnormal{z}}_{t-1})p({\mathbf{y}}_{t}|{\textnormal{z}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{z}}_{t}\sim p({\textnormal{z}}_{t}|{\textnormal{z}}_{t-1})p({\mathbf{y}}_{t}|{\textnormal{z}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textnormal{z}}_{t}\sim p({\textnormal{z}}_{t}|{\textnormal{z}}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{z}}_{t}\sim p({\textnormal{z}}_{t}|{\textnormal{z}}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> as implemented by Equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. That means Equations <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 A generative model of tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Compositional meta-learning through probabilistic task inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> are replaced by</p>
<table id="A1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{g}}_{t}=G_{\bm{\theta}}({\mathbf{x}}_{t},{\bm{g}}_{t-1},{\textnormal{z}}_{t-1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{g}}_{t}=G_{\bm{\theta}}({\mathbf{x}}_{t},{\bm{g}}_{t-1},{\textnormal{z}}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{m}}^{z_{t}}_{t}=M^{z_{t}}_{\bm{\phi}}({\mathbf{x}}_{t},{\bm{m}}_{t-1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒎</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒎</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{m}}^{z_{t}}_{t}=M^{z_{t}}_{\bm{\phi}}({\mathbf{x}}_{t},{\bm{m}}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{z_{t}}=L_{\text{Cat}}({\textnormal{z}}_{t};{\bm{W}}_{G}{\bm{g}}_{t})L_{\text{MVN}}({\mathbf{y}}_{t};{\bm{W}}_{M}{\bm{m}}^{z_{t}}_{t},\sigma{\bm{I}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>Cat</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>MVN</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒎</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{z_{t}}=L_{\text{Cat}}({\textnormal{z}}_{t};{\bm{W}}_{G}{\bm{g}}_{t})L_{\text{MVN}}({\mathbf{y}}_{t};{\bm{W}}_{M}{\bm{m}}^{z_{t}}_{t},\sigma{\bm{I}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textnormal{z}}_{t}\sim\text{Cat}(f_{z}/\sum_{i=1}^{N}f_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>z</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Cat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{z}}_{t}\sim\text{Cat}(f_{z}/\sum_{i=1}^{N}f_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{t}^{(k)}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}f^{(k)}_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{t}^{(k)}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}f^{(k)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{dist}}(x;\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>dist</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{dist}}(x;\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the likelihood of <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> under distribution <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dist" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dist</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. We use guided particle filtering only for the motor learning task in the current work, but it is task-agnostic.</p>
</div>
